## 3. Must-wire set (for AST shim Turn 2)

Turn 2 of the deep-dive plan (`audit/aarch64-deep-dive.oot:101-105`) replaces the text-output ladder arm with a direct `aarch64_compile_to_elf` call. The AST shim will live in `oodac/` and must wire to:

1. `aarch64_compile_to_elf` (`aarch64_codegen.oo:67`) replaces the .text+ELF emit arm. The current `aarch64_emit_linux` (`aarch64_emit_sys.oo:60`) is called from `aarch64_emit.oo:144` and prints GAS `.ascii` strings; the shim must instead call `aarch64_compile_to_elf` to return an ELF byte list.
2. `aarch64_compile_source` (`aarch64_codegen.oo:51`) replaces the source-to-text arm. The shim can call this to get just `.text` bytes (used as a building block by `aarch64_compile_to_elf`); the shim should reach this fn directly to avoid the linker when the caller only needs text.
3. `aarch64_link_executable` (`aarch64_linker.oo:114`) replaces the hand-rolled `printf '...' > out` ELF packer in `aarch64_emit_elf.oo:42-65`. The shim must call this to assemble a valid ELF header + program header.
4. `aarch64_lower_main` (`aarch64_lower.oo:199`) replaces the per-token `a64_collect_prints` harvest in `aarch64_emit.oo:143`. The shim must call this fn when the source matches the constrained `fn main` shape; it returns the lowered word list that `aarch64_compile_source` expects.
5. `aarch64_emit_prologue` + `aarch64_emit_epilogue` + `aarch64_emit_start` (`aarch64_codegen.oo:23,30,37`) replace the hand-rolled STP/MOV/RET/SVC sequence in `aarch64_emit_linux` (`aarch64_emit_sys.oo:77-104`). The shim must compose these to build a frame-correct `_start`+`main` block when not going through `aarch64_compile_to_elf`.
6. `aarch64_check_elf` (`aarch64_elf_check.oo:56`) replaces `aarch64_emit_diag_test` (`aarch64_emit.oo:129`) as the verification arm. The shim must call this on the produced byte list before passing it to the writer; the diag gate today is shape-level, not byte-level.
7. `arm64_words_to_bytes` + `arm64_concat` (`aarch64_encoder.oo:33,22`) are the byte-packing utilities. The shim must use these when composing the prologue+main+epilogue byte stream from individual word lists.
8. `abi_fp_reg`/`abi_lr_reg`/`abi_sp_reg`/`abi_sys_reg` (`aarch64_abi.oo:35-47`) are the AAPCS64 constants. The shim must use these instead of hard-coded register indices when building prologue/epilogue/start so a future ABI bump does not break the shim.
9. `aarch64_disasm_inst` (`aarch64_disasm.oo:174`) is the word -> mnemonic disassembler. The shim must call this in the verification path; today `aarch64_emit_linux` has no disassembly self-check, so a wrong opcode silently ships as GAS text.
10. `aarch64_suite_has_main` + `aarch64_suite_pass_line` + `aarch64_compile_suite_elf` (`aarch64_suite.oo:41,80,166`) are the suite gate. The shim must call these when the input source has a "PASS" string marker, so the suite's "expected exit 0" assertion is enforced by the shim rather than by the hand-rolled `_start`.

## 4. Rename set

The shim layer should rewrite calls to use a consistent `a64_*` prefix matching `aarch64_syscalls.oo`, `aarch64_elf_check.oo`, and `aarch64_qemu_exec.oo`, instead of the `aarch64_*` prefix used in `aarch64_codegen.oo`, `aarch64_lower.oo`, `aarch64_disasm.oo`, `aarch64_rec.oo`, `aarch64_reg_graph.oo`, `aarch64_suite.oo`, `aarch64_matrix_names.oo`. The x86 audit (`audit/x86-std-audit.oot:51`) confirms the same pattern: x86 uses `x86_*` and short `x86_*`; aarch64 should settle on `a64_*`.

1. `aarch64_compile_to_elf` -> `a64_compile_to_elf` (called at `aarch64_emit_elf.oo:71`, `tests/test_challenger_pipeline.oo:25`).
2. `aarch64_compile_source` -> `a64_compile_source` (called at `aarch64_codegen.oo:58,68,84`).
3. `aarch64_compile_program_elf` -> `a64_compile_program_elf` (called at `aarch64_codegen.oo:76`; today 1 external caller).
4. `aarch64_compile_unit_to_elf` -> `a64_compile_unit_to_elf` (called at `aarch64_codegen.oo:82`; 18+ external callers).
5. `aarch64_compile_text_to_elf` -> `a64_compile_text_to_elf` (called at `aarch64_codegen.oo:90,96,100,104`).
6. `aarch64_compile_recursive_elf` / `_match_elf` / `_array_elf` -> `a64_compile_recursive_elf` / `_match_elf` / `_array_elf` (1 external caller each).
7. `aarch64_emit_prologue` / `_epilogue` / `_start` / `_sample_src` -> `a64_emit_prologue` / `_epilogue` / `_start` / `_sample_src`.
8. `aarch64_lower_main` -> `a64_lower_main` (called at `aarch64_codegen.oo:52`).
9. `aarch64_link_executable` -> `a64_link_executable` (called at `aarch64_codegen.oo:72,92`).
10. `aarch64_disasm_inst` / `aarch64_disasm_stream` -> `a64_disasm_inst` / `a64_disasm_stream` (called at `aarch64_disasm.oo:192`; 21+ external sites).
11. `aarch64_sys_read` / `_write` / `_close` / `_mmap` / `_exit` -> `a64_sys_read` / `_write` / `_close` / `_mmap` / `_exit`.
12. `aarch64_emit_svc0` / `aarch64_emit_sys_write` / `_read` / `_close` / `_mmap` / `_exit` / `aarch64_emit_stdout_write` -> `a64_emit_svc0` / `a64_emit_sys_*` / `a64_emit_stdout_write`.
13. `aarch64_emit_recursive_body` / `_match_body` / `_struct_array_body` -> `a64_emit_recursive_body` / `_match_body` / `_struct_array_body`.
14. `aarch64_aligned_push` / `aarch64_rec_align` / `aarch64_body_frame` -> `a64_aligned_push` / `a64_rec_align` / `a64_body_frame`.
15. `aarch64_alloc_color` / `aarch64_color_of` / `aarch64_is_spilled` / `aarch64_reg_k` -> `a64_alloc_color` / `a64_color_of` / `a64_is_spilled` / `a64_reg_k`.
16. `aarch64_matrix_count` / `_tag` / `_path` / `_qemu_req` / `_is_orch` -> `a64_matrix_count` / `_tag` / `_path` / `_qemu_req` / `_is_orch`.
17. `aarch64_suite_has_main` / `_pass_line` / `aarch64_compile_suite_elf` -> `a64_suite_has_main` / `_pass_line` / `a64_compile_suite_elf`.

## 5. Dead symbols (zero external callers)

The following pub fns in `std/hw/arch_aarch64/` have zero callers outside the std directory itself. They are kept for self-consistency or for tests.

1. `arm64_asm_new` (`aarch64_assembler.oo:18`) plus `arm64_asm_emit_inst` (`aarch64_assembler.oo:26`), `arm64_asm_byte_count` (`aarch64_assembler.oo:43`), `arm64_asm_finish` (`aarch64_assembler.oo:47`). The `Arm64Assembler` struct and all 4 ops are touched only by `std_qa_compiler_triad.oo:59-68` and `std_qa_arch_native.oo:70-76`; the main codegen path uses `arm64_words_to_bytes` directly. The struct is dead weight in the emit hot path.
2. `neon_word_add_2d`/`sub_2d`/`mul_2d`/`and_16b`/`eor_16b`/`fmla_4s`/`ldp_q`/`stp_q` and 8 `neon_encode_*`/`neon_rd`/`neon_rn`/`neon_rm`/`neon_rt`/`neon_rt2`/`neon_is_vreg` (`aarch64_neon.oo:15-113`). All 17 pub fns are called only from `std_qa_aarch64_torture.oo:48-60`. No x-pipeline or main-codegen path uses NEON.
3. `aarch64_compile_program_elf` (`aarch64_codegen.oo:172`). The 253-line `aarch64_program.oo` is reachable only from this fn; external callers = 1 (a stress test).
4. `aarch64_compile_suite_elf` (`aarch64_suite.oo:166`) plus `aarch64_suite_has_main` (`aarch64_suite.oo:41`) and `aarch64_suite_pass_line` (`aarch64_suite.oo:80`). 0 external callers; `aarch64_compile_suite_elf` is the only consumer of the other two.
5. `aarch64_emit_recursive_body` (`aarch64_rec.oo:57`), `aarch64_emit_match_body` (`aarch64_rec.oo:74`), `aarch64_emit_struct_array_body` (`aarch64_rec.oo:95`), `aarch64_body_frame` (`aarch64_rec.oo:114`). 0 external callers; the only consumer is `aarch64_codegen.oo:96,100,104`.
6. `a64_elf_to_octal` (`aarch64_qemu_exec.oo:20`) and `a64_write_and_chmod` (`aarch64_qemu_exec.oo:57`). Only `aarch64_qemu_exec.oo:73,59` call these internally; no external caller imports `aarch64_qemu_exec.oo` for octal/write. (`a64_elf_to_octal` has a twin at `oodac/aarch64_emit_elf.oo:19` that is the only oodac consumer; the std one is a parallel duplicate.)
7. The full `a64x_*` (aarch64_xarith, xclosure, xcond, xexpr, xfloat, xfn, xloop, xmark, xsimple, xstate, xstmt, xterm, xtypes, xunit, xscan) and `a64rt_*` (aarch64_xrt + 16 xrt_*.oo) families: 0 external callers. The 22 x-pipeline files and 19 xrt-runtime files exist only to feed `aarch64_program.oo:146-170`. The shim is the first oodac/ caller; today the whole AST pipeline is dormant.

## 6. Surface-area risks

1. **Single import path between oodac/ and std/hw/arch_aarch64/**. Only `oodac/aarch64_emit_elf.oo:13` imports from std today. The shim will become the second import edge. Risk: a refactor that moves `aarch64_compile_to_elf` into a new module forces the shim to follow; a follow-up that splits `aarch64_codegen.oo` into a thin facade + a multi-fn compiler leaves the shim holding a stale import path.
2. **Two naming conventions in the same std tree** (`aarch64_*` in `aarch64_codegen.oo`, `aarch64_lower.oo`, `aarch64_rec.oo`, `aarch64_reg_graph.oo`, `aarch64_suite.oo`, `aarch64_matrix_names.oo`, `aarch64_disasm.oo`; `a64_*` in `aarch64_syscalls.oo`, `aarch64_elf_check.oo`, `aarch64_qemu_exec.oo`, `aarch64_linker.oo`). The shim must pick one; the rename set in §4 is the recommendation.
3. **Two parallel ELF packers** (`aarch64_link_executable` in std vs `a64_write_elf_bytes` + `a64_elf_to_octal` in `oodac/aarch64_emit_elf.oo:34,19`). The shim must not import the oodac twin. Today both packers emit `EM_AARCH64=183`; if a future patch changes `e_machine` to `e_machine_aarch64()` lookup (an ELF-helper constant added in `aarch64_linker.oo:86`), the oodac twin will not see it.
4. **Dual-path codegen drift** (per `audit/aarch64-deep-dive.oot:74-88`). `aarch64_compile_source` (`aarch64_codegen.oo:51`) and `aarch64_emit_linux` (`aarch64_emit_sys.oo:60`) emit `.text` independently. A 64-bit-immediate fix to one is invisible to the other. The shim must close this gap by calling only `aarch64_compile_to_elf` and dropping `aarch64_emit_linux` entirely (Turn 2 acceptance).
5. **`aarch64_xterm.oo` 258 lines**. The file law is `<= 256`. The x-pipeline is the AST shim's biggest target; a shim edit will need to either split `aarch64_xterm.oo` first or never re-export its `a64x_*` symbols.
6. **No qemu gate in the text path**. `aarch64_emit_linux` has no `aarch64_check_elf` call and no `aarch64_disasm_inst` call. The shim's §3 must add both; otherwise a wrong opcode ships as GAS text that is then assembled and qemu-tested downstream only, where the failure is harder to localize.
7. **`aarch64_emit_prologue` is unused outside `aarch64_codegen.oo`** (called only at `aarch64_codegen.oo:25`). Once the shim calls it directly, it becomes a stable surface; today it is internal-by-convention only.
8. **258 lines in `aarch64_xterm.oo` and the file law**. Splitting risk. Not blocking, but a shim that grows a new arm may push a second file over the line.
